ca = {"iso2": "ca", "name": "Canada", "tier": "emerging",
 "tagline": "A cold-climate wine country that turned its hardest winters into icewine, and is now learning to make Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Cabernet Franc to match.",
 "stats": {"vineyard_ha": 12000, "production_mhl": 0.6, "year": 2015, "world_rank_production": None,
           "note": "Approximate national totals from 2015 industry figures (Wikipedia, Canadian wine: 12,150 ha, 56 million litres, 548 wineries). Updates: Ontario about 13,600 acres in the Niagara Peninsula alone (OWAA 2024 to 2025); BC 8,686 insured wine-grape acres in 2026 (BC Ministry of Agriculture, July 2026). OIV does not list Canada among major producers; it records Canadian consumption of 2.8 mhl in 2025."},
 "overview": "Canada grows wine grapes in four main provinces: Ontario and British Columbia make nearly all of it, with smaller and fast-growing industries in Quebec and Nova Scotia. Almost every Canadian vineyard sits within a few kilometres of a large body of water (Lake Ontario, Lake Erie, Okanagan Lake, the Bay of Fundy, the St. Lawrence), because water is what makes viticulture possible this far north. Lakes store summer heat, slow spring budbreak until frost danger has passed, and soften the deep winter cold that can kill vines.\n\nThat cold is also Canada's signature. Canada is the world's largest producer of icewine, made from grapes left to freeze on the vine and picked at minus 8 degrees Celsius or below; about 90 percent of it comes from Ontario. Inniskillin's 1989 Vidal icewine won the Grand Prix d'Honneur at Vinexpo in Bordeaux in 1991 and announced Canada to the wine world. Since then, the quality wines have shifted toward dry styles: Niagara Chardonnay, Riesling and Cabernet Franc; Prince Edward County Pinot Noir grown on limestone; Okanagan Merlot, Syrah and Bordeaux blends from desert benches; Quebec hybrids; Nova Scotia's crisp Tidal Bay whites and traditional-method sparkling.\n\nCanada's appellation system is provincial, not federal. Ontario and British Columbia use the Vintners Quality Alliance (VQA): wines must be made entirely from grapes grown in the province and pass a tasting panel, and appellation names carry minimum-origin rules. Quebec has protected geographical indications (IGP Vin du Québec, plus IGPs for icewine and ice cider), and Nova Scotia has its Tidal Bay appellation. A long-running anomaly is 'International-Canadian Blends' (formerly 'Cellared in Canada'), wines bottled in Canada largely from imported bulk wine, which outsell VQA wine on many shelves.\n\nRecent years have been hard. Deep freezes in December 2022 and January 2024 destroyed most of the Okanagan's 2024 crop and forced growers to pull and replant thousands of acres. In 2025, US tariffs prompted Canadian provinces to remove American wine from liquor-board shelves, a sudden opening for domestic producers.",
 "history": [
  T("c. 1000", "Vinland", "Norse sagas name the land Leif Erikson reached 'Vinland' for its wild grapes or berries; L'Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland is the only confirmed Norse site.", 51.5954, -55.5333),
  T("1811", "Johann Schiller at Cooksville", "Johann Schiller, a German-born former soldier, is said to have made wine from wild and domesticated labrusca vines on the Credit River west of Toronto around 1811; he is often called the father of Canadian wine. The Comte de Courtenay later expanded the vineyard as Clair House, which showed wine in Paris in 1867.", 43.583, -79.624),
  T("1859", "Father Pandosy's mission vineyard", "Oblate missionary Charles Pandosy plants vines at his mission near present-day Kelowna to make sacramental wine, the first in the Okanagan.", 49.861, -119.456),
  T("1866", "Vin Villa on Pelee Island", "Kentucky planters found Vin Villa on Pelee Island in Lake Erie, Canada's first commercial winery.", 41.795, -82.66),
  T("1916 to 1927", "Prohibition and the licence freeze", "Provincial prohibition (Ontario 1916 to 1927) exempted native wine, so wineries multiplied; afterward Ontario's Liquor Control Board froze new winery licences, and none was issued in Niagara from 1929 until 1975.", None, None),
  T("1975", "Inniskillin", "Donald Ziraldo and Karl Kaiser receive the first new Ontario winery licence since 1929 and found Inniskillin at Niagara-on-the-Lake.", 43.2107, -79.0637),
  T("1988 to 1990", "Free trade and the VQA", "The 1988 Canada-US Free Trade Agreement removes protection for Canadian wine; growers are paid to pull out labrusca and hybrid vines and replant vinifera. Ontario launches the voluntary VQA in 1988 and BC follows in 1990.", None, None),
  T("1991", "Icewine at Vinexpo", "Inniskillin 1989 Vidal Icewine wins the Grand Prix d'Honneur at Vinexpo in Bordeaux, Canada's first major international wine award.", None, None),
  T("1999 to 2001", "VQA becomes law; EU opens to icewine", "Ontario's VQA Act (1999) makes VQA the provincial wine authority; in 2001 the EU accepts Canadian icewine standards and allows imports.", None, None),
  T("2015 to 2022", "Sub-appellations in BC", "Golden Mile Bench becomes BC's first sub-GI (2015); Okanagan Falls, Naramata Bench, Skaha Bench and others follow, and six more are added in June 2022.", 49.12, -119.58),
  T("2024", "The Okanagan freeze", "A January 2024 cold snap, after damage in December 2022, destroys nearly the whole 2024 Okanagan crop; most damaged acres are replanted by 2026.", 49.25, -119.55)],
 "classification": "Canada has no federal appellation system; wine origin is a provincial matter. Ontario: VQA Ontario (Vintners Quality Alliance Act, 1999) recognises three primary appellations (Niagara Peninsula, Lake Erie North Shore, Prince Edward County), two regional appellations within the Niagara Peninsula (Niagara-on-the-Lake and Niagara Escarpment) and 11 sub-appellations (10 in Niagara plus South Islands). VQA wines must be 100 percent Ontario-grown, at least 85 percent from a named appellation, and pass laboratory and tasting tests. British Columbia: BC VQA and the BC Wine Authority recognise the British Columbia GI, nine regional GIs and 12 sub-GIs (11 in the Okanagan plus Cowichan Valley); wines naming a GI need at least 95 percent of grapes from it. Icewine everywhere must come from grapes naturally frozen on the vine, picked at minus 8 degrees Celsius or colder, with at least 35 degrees Brix. Quebec: the IGP Vin du Québec covers wines from 100 percent Quebec grapes; separate IGPs protect Vin de glace du Québec and Cidre de glace du Québec. Nova Scotia: Tidal Bay is a provincial appellation-style designation for blended whites. 'International-Canadian Blends' may contain mostly imported wine.",
 "climate_soils": "Short, warm growing seasons and dangerous winters. Niagara and Lake Erie North Shore rely on the Great Lakes and on the Niagara Escarpment, which traps lake air against the benches; soils are glacial clays, silts and limestone-derived tills. Prince Edward County has shallow clay loam over fractured limestone, and growers often bury vines under soil for winter. The southern Okanagan is the northern tip of the Sonoran-type desert ecosystem, with under 300 mm of rain and very hot days; glacial benches of sand and gravel. Quebec's continental winters force hybrids and protective covers; Nova Scotia is cool and maritime.",
 "grapes": [{"name": "Vidal Blanc", "note": "Thick-skinned hybrid that is the backbone of Ontario icewine."},
            {"name": "Riesling", "note": "Niagara's most consistent dry and icewine variety."},
            {"name": "Chardonnay", "note": "Niagara benches and Prince Edward County; the focus of the i4C cool-climate Chardonnay festival."},
            {"name": "Cabernet Franc", "note": "Ontario's best red and an icewine grape."},
            {"name": "Pinot Noir", "note": "Prince Edward County, Niagara, Okanagan Kelowna area and Vancouver Island."},
            {"name": "Merlot", "note": "The Okanagan's most important red."},
            {"name": "Pinot Gris", "note": "BC's most planted white."},
            {"name": "L'Acadie Blanc", "note": "Hybrid bred at Vineland, Ontario, now Nova Scotia's signature grape."},
            {"name": "Frontenac, Marquette, Seyval Blanc", "note": "Cold-hardy hybrids for Quebec."}],
 "regions": [ON, "Niagara Peninsula", BC, "Okanagan Valley", QC, NS],
 "further_reading": ["The World of Canadian Wine, John Schreiner", "The Wine Atlas of Canada, Tony Aspler", "Icewine: The Complete Story, John Schreiner", "Wines of Canada, Rod Phillips"],
 "sources": [{"title": t, "url": u} for t, u in SRC_ON + SRC_BC + SRC_QC + SRC_NS] + [{"title": "OIV, State of the World Wine Sector in 2025", "url": "https://www.oiv.int/sites/default/files/2026-05/OIV-State_of_the_World_Wine_Sector_in_2025.pdf"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Canadian wine", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canadian_wine"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Ice wine", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_wine"}]}

regions = [
 region("ca", ON, 43.4, -79.6, 6.4, "Lake-moderated vineyards on the Niagara benches, Lake Erie's warm shore and Prince Edward County's limestone.",
  "Ontario makes about 60 percent of Canadian wine from about 17,000 acres (6,900 ha) of vines in three appellations between 41 and 44 degrees north, the same latitude band as Tuscany and Oregon but with far colder winters. Niagara Peninsula is the heart (105 wineries and 13,600 acres in 2024 to 2025); Lake Erie North Shore is the warmest; Prince Edward County is the coldest and most terroir-obsessed. Ontario produces about 90 percent of Canada's icewine.",
  "Johann Schiller's Credit River vineyard (around 1811) and Pelee Island's Vin Villa (1866) were the first. T.G. Bright and Company (1874) became a giant of sweet, fortified 'port' and 'sherry' from labrusca. Prohibition (1916 to 1927) exempted Ontario wine, and dozens of wineries sprang up; the Liquor Control Board then froze licences. Inniskillin's 1975 licence and Château des Charmes (1978, Paul Bosc, first all-vinifera estate in Niagara) began the modern era; VQA followed in 1988.",
  "Lake Ontario and the 'lake effect' along the Niagara Escarpment, Lake Erie's shallow, warm water, and the limestone of Prince Edward County. Glacial lake beds (Lake Iroquois) left benches of clay, silt and dolomitic limestone-derived soils.",
  "Riesling, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Vidal, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Gris, Gewürztraminer.",
  "Icewine; dry and off-dry Riesling; mineral Chardonnay; Cabernet Franc; Gamay; traditional-method sparkling.",
  "VQA Ontario: three primary appellations, two regional appellations, 11 sub-appellations.",
  ["Niagara Peninsula", "Lake Erie North Shore", "South Islands", "Prince Edward County"], SRC_ON),
 region("ca", "Niagara Peninsula", 43.15, -79.30, 9.0, "Canada's largest wine region, squeezed between Lake Ontario and the Niagara Escarpment.",
  "The Niagara Peninsula appellation runs about 55 km from Grimsby to the Niagara River. The Niagara Escarpment, a limestone cliff that continues to Niagara Falls, traps air off Lake Ontario: in spring the cool lake delays budbreak, in autumn the warm lake delays frost, and on winter nights air circulating along the escarpment keeps the benches warmer than the plain. The appellation averages about 1,590 growing degree days (Celsius). Ten sub-appellations, grouped into two regional appellations, divide benchland from lakeshore plain.",
  "Inniskillin (1975), Château des Charmes (1978) and Cave Spring (1986) proved vinifera could thrive here. Tawse, Hidden Bench, Le Clos Jordanne (Burgundian collaboration with Boisset, 2003 to 2012, revived by Arterra) and Southbrook (biodynamic) define the modern style.",
  "Benchlands of the escarpment: limestone, dolomite and shale-derived clays, well drained, with cooling breezes. Lake plain: deeper, heavier clay and silt over the old Lake Iroquois bed, warmer and earlier ripening near the lake.",
  "Riesling, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Vidal.",
  "Bench Riesling and Chardonnay; Cabernet Franc and Bordeaux blends from Niagara-on-the-Lake; icewine.",
  "Niagara Peninsula appellation; regional appellations Niagara-on-the-Lake (four sub-appellations) and Niagara Escarpment (three); three other sub-appellations (Creek Shores, Lincoln Lakeshore, Vinemount Ridge).",
  ["Niagara Peninsula", "Niagara-on-the-Lake", "Niagara Escarpment", "Niagara River", "Niagara Lakeshore", "Four Mile Creek", "St. David's Bench", "Short Hills Bench", "Twenty Mile Bench", "Beamsville Bench", "Creek Shores", "Lincoln Lakeshore", "Vinemount Ridge"], SRC_ON, parent=ON),
 region("ca", BC, 49.7, -120.5, 6.2, "Desert benches above glacial lakes, island vineyards and mountain valleys at the edge of viable viticulture.",
  "British Columbia has around 10,000 acres of wine grapes and more than 300 wineries, most of them small. The Okanagan Valley holds about 90 percent of the vineyards and 95 percent of output; the rest are in the Similkameen, on Vancouver Island and the Gulf Islands, in the Fraser Valley and in the new interior GIs (Thompson Valley, Shuswap, Lillooet, Kootenays). In 2026 the top varieties by insured acreage were Pinot Noir, Merlot, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Pinot Gris and Cabernet Sauvignon.",
  "Father Pandosy planted mission vines near Kelowna in 1859; Calona Wines (1932) is BC's oldest continuously operating winery. After the 1988 Free Trade Agreement growers pulled out most of their hybrid vineyards with government help and replanted with vinifera, and BC VQA began in 1990. Nk'Mip Cellars (2002), owned by the Osoyoos Indian Band, is often cited as the first Indigenous-owned winery in North America. Cold snaps in December 2022 and January 2024 destroyed nearly the whole 2024 crop, forcing about 1,970 acres to be removed in 2025, almost all of them replanted in 2026.",
  "Glacial terraces (benches) of sand, gravel and silt above long, deep lakes; the south is semi-arid with under 300 mm of rain, very hot summer days and cool nights. Winter lows below minus 20 degrees Celsius are the main hazard.",
  "Pinot Noir, Merlot, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Pinot Gris, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Riesling, Gewürztraminer.",
  "Bordeaux-style blends and Syrah from the south Okanagan; Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and Riesling from the north and the coast; icewine.",
  "BC Wine Authority: British Columbia GI, nine regional GIs, 12 sub-GIs (wines naming a GI need 95 percent of grapes from it).",
  ["Okanagan Valley", "Similkameen Valley", "Fraser Valley", "Vancouver Island", "Cowichan Valley", "Gulf Islands", "Lillooet", "Thompson Valley", "Shuswap", "Kootenays"], SRC_BC),
 region("ca", "Okanagan Valley", 49.55, -119.6, 8.0, "A 200 km string of lakes from the Sonoran-edge desert at Osoyoos to the cool orchards of Lake Country.",
  "The Okanagan Valley GI runs about 175 km north to south along a chain of glacial lakes (Okanagan, Skaha, Vaseux, Osoyoos). The south around Oliver and Osoyoos is the hottest, driest corner of Canada, where the Black Sage Bench and Golden Mile Bench grow Merlot, Cabernet, Syrah and Malbec; the north around Kelowna and Lake Country is cooler and suits Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling and sparkling wine. Eleven sub-GIs subdivide it (Golden Mile Bench 2015; Okanagan Falls 2018; Naramata Bench and Skaha Bench 2019; Golden Mile Slopes; six more in 2022).",
  "Pandosy's 1859 mission vineyard, Calona (1932), the post-1988 vinifera replanting, and an explosion of estate wineries after 2000 (Mission Hill, Burrowing Owl, Quails' Gate, Osoyoos Larose, Painted Rock, Blue Mountain).",
  "Glacial benches of sand, gravel and silt, alluvial fans and some granite; large lakes moderate temperatures; rainfall below 250 mm near Osoyoos to about 400 mm in the north.",
  "Merlot, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Cabernet Franc, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Riesling, Gewürztraminer.",
  "From ripe Bordeaux blends and Syrah to taut Pinot Noir, Riesling and sparkling.",
  "Okanagan Valley GI and 11 sub-GIs.",
  ["Okanagan Valley", "Golden Mile Bench", "Golden Mile Slopes", "Okanagan Falls", "Skaha Bench", "Naramata Bench", "Summerland Bench", "Summerland Lakefront", "Summerland Valleys", "South Kelowna Slopes", "East Kelowna Slopes", "Lake Country"], SRC_BC, parent=BC),
 region("ca", QC, 45.3, -72.9, 7.4, "Hybrids under winter blankets, and the birthplace of ice cider.",
  "Quebec's wine industry is young, cold and fast-growing, concentrated south of Montreal in Montérégie and the Eastern Townships (Brome-Missisquoi around Dunham is the historic heart), with vineyards also near Quebec City and on Île d'Orléans. Winters reach minus 30 degrees Celsius, so growers rely on cold-hardy hybrids (Frontenac, Marquette, Vidal, Seyval Blanc, Vandal-Cliche, Sainte-Croix) or protect vinifera by hilling soil over the trunks and covering rows with geotextile fabric. The IGP Vin du Québec requires 100 percent Quebec grapes.",
  "Jacques Cartier found wild vines on Île d'Orléans in 1535 and called it Île de Bacchus; Samuel de Champlain's European vines died in 1608. The modern industry began in the early 1980s around Dunham: Christian Barthomeuf planted Domaine des Côtes d'Ardoise in 1980, and L'Orpailleur was founded in 1982. Barthomeuf also created ice cider (cidre de glace) around 1989, now a Quebec speciality with its own IGP.",
  "Glacial and slate-derived soils in the Appalachian foothills; continental climate with hot summers, short seasons and severe winters.",
  "Frontenac (noir, gris, blanc), Marquette, Vidal, Seyval Blanc, Vandal-Cliche, Sainte-Croix, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir under cover.",
  "Crisp whites, rosé, light reds, icewine, ice cider.",
  "IGP Vin du Québec; IGP Vin de glace du Québec; IGP Cidre de glace du Québec.",
  ["Vin du Québec", "Vin de glace du Québec", "Cidre de glace du Québec"], SRC_QC),
 region("ca", NS, 45.05, -64.4, 7.6, "Tidal valleys on the Bay of Fundy, crisp whites and world-class sparkling wine.",
  "Nova Scotia has a few hundred hectares of vines and around 20 to 30 wineries, mostly in the Annapolis and Gaspereau valleys near the Bay of Fundy, whose enormous tides moderate temperatures. The province's identity rests on two styles: Tidal Bay, a crisp, low-alcohol blended white appellation (first vintage 2012), and traditional-method sparkling wine, led by Benjamin Bridge. L'Acadie Blanc, a hybrid bred in Ontario, is the signature grape.",
  "Governor Isaac de Razilly wrote in 1634 that 'Bordeaux vines' were doing well at La Hève (LaHave); the Acadians grew grapes in the 17th century. Roger Dial founded Grand Pré Winery in 1979; Jost Vineyards (1983) at Malagash followed.",
  "Cool maritime climate, short season; soils of sandstone, shale and glacial till.",
  "L'Acadie Blanc, Seyval Blanc, Vidal, Geisenheim 318, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Marechal Foch, Lucie Kuhlmann.",
  "Tidal Bay white blends; traditional-method sparkling; fruit wines.", "Tidal Bay appellation (provincial standard, tasting panel approval).",
  ["Tidal Bay"], SRC_NS)]

A = []
NP = "Niagara Peninsula"
A.append(app("ca", "Niagara Peninsula", "VQA", ON, "Canada's largest appellation and the source of most Ontario VQA wine: 13,600 acres and 105 wineries in 2024 to 2025. The Niagara Escarpment and Lake Ontario create a protected corridor where over 40 varieties ripen. Benchland vineyards along the escarpment give the most structured Riesling and Chardonnay; the warmer lake plain gives Cabernet Franc, Merlot and much of Ontario's icewine.", created="1988", styles=["white", "red", "sweet", "sparkling", "rose"], dominant_style="white", grapes="Riesling, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Gamay, Vidal", producers=["Inniskillin", "Château des Charmes", "Cave Spring", "Tawse", "Hidden Bench", "Southbrook", "Stratus", "Pearl Morissette", "Henry of Pelham"], contains=["Niagara-on-the-Lake", "Niagara Escarpment", "Creek Shores", "Lincoln Lakeshore", "Vinemount Ridge"]))
A.append(app("ca", "Niagara-on-the-Lake", "VQA", ON, "A regional appellation covering the plain below the escarpment between St. Catharines and the Niagara River, with four sub-appellations. It is the warmest, most-visited part of Niagara (39 wineries, about 1,597 growing degree days) and the heartland of Ontario icewine and Bordeaux reds.", subregion="Niagara Peninsula", styles=["red", "white", "sweet"], dominant_style="red", grapes="Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Riesling, Vidal", producers=["Inniskillin", "Stratus", "Château des Charmes", "Peller", "Southbrook"], contains=["Niagara River", "Niagara Lakeshore", "Four Mile Creek", "St. David's Bench"]))
A.append(app("ca", "Niagara Escarpment", "VQA", ON, "A regional appellation joining the three bench sub-appellations (Short Hills, Twenty Mile, Beamsville) along the escarpment between St. Catharines and Grimsby, with 25 wineries. Fossil-rich limestone and dolomite soils, slopes and lake breezes give Ontario's most mineral Riesling and Chardonnay. Not to be confused with the US Niagara Escarpment AVA across the river in New York.", subregion="Niagara Peninsula", styles=["white", "red", "sparkling"], dominant_style="white", grapes="Chardonnay, Riesling, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Franc, Gamay", producers=["Tawse", "Cave Spring", "Hidden Bench", "Thirty Bench", "Flat Rock", "Pearl Morissette"], contains=["Short Hills Bench", "Twenty Mile Bench", "Beamsville Bench"]))
for nm, lat, lon, txt, prod in [
 ("Niagara River", 43.18, -79.05, "Sub-appellation (Niagara-on-the-Lake) along the Niagara River, where the deep, fast-moving river carries warm air and keeps frost away; about 1,606 growing degree days and 199 frost-free days. Warm enough for Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot as well as icewine.", ["Inniskillin", "Reif Estate", "Riverview"]),
 ("Niagara Lakeshore", 43.245, -79.11, "Sub-appellation on the shore of Lake Ontario around the town of Niagara-on-the-Lake, with the longest frost-free season in Niagara (about 213 days) and around 1,563 growing degree days; lake breezes keep it frost-free late into autumn.", ["Konzelmann", "Strewn"]),
 ("Four Mile Creek", 43.20, -79.15, "Sub-appellation on the flat plain of Niagara-on-the-Lake around Four Mile Creek, with heavy clay-loam soils and about 1,582 growing degree days; warm, early-ripening sites for Cabernet Franc, Merlot and icewine Vidal.", ["Château des Charmes (estate parcels)", "Peller", "Jackson-Triggs"]),
 ("St. David's Bench", 43.155, -79.12, "A raised bench at the foot of the escarpment around St. Davids, with the highest heat accumulation in Niagara (about 1,637 growing degree days); well-drained, sloping soils over the escarpment's lower terrace. Château des Charmes's château stands here.", ["Château des Charmes", "Ravine Vineyard", "Two Sisters"]),
 ("Short Hills Bench", 43.11, -79.30, "The easternmost escarpment bench, a hilly area southwest of St. Catharines around Twelve Mile Creek; relatively far from the lake, it has hot summers but fewer frost-free days (about 187). Henry of Pelham's family estate dates to a land grant of 1794.", ["Henry of Pelham", "Hinterbrook"]),
 ("Twenty Mile Bench", 43.15, -79.39, "A bench sub-appellation around Jordan and Vineland, cut by Twenty Mile Creek ravines, with steep, limestone-rich slopes and cooling breezes (about 1,523 growing degree days). Home of Cave Spring (1986) and Tawse and some of Ontario's best Riesling and Chardonnay.", ["Cave Spring", "Tawse", "Flat Rock", "Malivoire", "Le Clos Jordanne (sourcing)"]),
 ("Beamsville Bench", 43.155, -79.49, "The western escarpment bench around Beamsville, with steep, northward-facing slopes, well-drained clay-loam over dolomitic limestone and the most frost-free days on the escarpment (about 217); 12 wineries. Known for taut Riesling, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir.", ["Hidden Bench", "Thirty Bench", "Rosewood", "Fielding (near)", "Bachelder (sourcing)"]),
 ("Creek Shores", 43.18, -79.32, "A lakeshore-plain sub-appellation between St. Catharines and Jordan, crossed by creeks (Twenty Mile, Fifteen Mile, Sixteen Mile) that drain the escarpment; about 1,550 growing degree days. Lake Ontario and the creeks moderate temperatures.", ["Harbour Estates", "Rockway"]),
 ("Lincoln Lakeshore", 43.19, -79.45, "A flat, lake-cooled sub-appellation along Lake Ontario in the Town of Lincoln, with a long frost-free season (about 198 days) but cooler summers (about 1,527 growing degree days); it supplies grapes to many Niagara wineries.", ["Kacaba (nearby)", "growers"]),
 ("Vinemount Ridge", 43.13, -79.47, "The highest and coolest Niagara sub-appellation, on the top ridge of the escarpment above the benches, with the shortest frost-free season (about 178 days) and lowest heat (about 1,469 growing degree days). Riesling and icewine thrive; cold winters are a risk.", ["Kew Vineyards", "Di Profio"])]:
    sub = "Niagara-on-the-Lake" if nm in ("Niagara River", "Niagara Lakeshore", "Four Mile Creek", "St. David's Bench") else ("Niagara Escarpment" if nm in ("Short Hills Bench", "Twenty Mile Bench", "Beamsville Bench") else NP)
    d = app("ca", nm, "VQA", ON, txt, subregion=sub, styles=["white", "red", "sweet"], dominant_style="white" if "Bench" in nm or nm == "Vinemount Ridge" else "red", producers=prod, match=[nm, nm.replace("'", "’")])
    d["lat"], d["lon"] = lat, lon
    A.append(d)
A.append(app("ca", "Lake Erie North Shore", "VQA", ON, "Ontario's southernmost and warmest mainland appellation, a bow-shaped strip along Lake Erie from Amherstburg to near St. Thomas at about 42 degrees north. Shallow Lake Erie warms quickly, giving more growing degree days than any other Ontario region; red Bordeaux varieties ripen well. Colio Estate (1980) at Harrow is the oldest modern winery; about 15 wineries.", created="1988", styles=["red", "white", "sweet"], dominant_style="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Riesling, Chardonnay, Vidal", producers=["Colio Estate", "Pelee Island Winery", "Cooper's Hawk", "Oxley Estate"], contains=["South Islands"]))
A.append(app("ca", "South Islands", "VQA", ON, "A sub-appellation (July 2015) covering the nine islands of the Township of Pelee in Lake Erie, at Canada's southernmost point. Grapes grow mainly on Pelee Island, about 20 km offshore, where the growing season can be 30 days longer than on the mainland and harvest begins in August. Vin Villa, Canada's first commercial winery, opened here in 1866; Pelee Island Winery farms the island's vineyards on sandy loam and clay over limestone.", subregion="Lake Erie North Shore", created="2015", styles=["red", "white", "sweet"], dominant_style="red", grapes="Cabernet Franc, Pinot Noir, Riesling, Chardonnay, Tempranillo, Vidal", producers=["Pelee Island Winery"]))
A.append(app("ca", "Prince Edward County", "VQA", ON, "Ontario's newest and coolest primary appellation (2007), an island-like county in Lake Ontario east of Toronto. Shallow clay loam over fractured Trenton limestone drew winemakers looking for Burgundian conditions; the downside is winter cold so severe that many growers bury their vines under soil each autumn and dig them out in spring. Pinot Noir and Chardonnay are taut, mineral and low in alcohol; Norman Hardie, Closson Chase, Hinterland (sparkling) and Rosehall Run are leading names.", created="2007", styles=["red", "white", "sparkling", "rose"], dominant_style="red", grapes="Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Riesling, Cabernet Franc", producers=["Norman Hardie", "Closson Chase", "Hinterland", "Rosehall Run", "Trail Estate", "Grange of Prince Edward"]))
